        "content": "{\n  \"rule\": \"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.\",\n  \"text\": \"The baby monitor began picking up my mother three days after her funeral.\\n\\nNot her voice. Her cough: two short barks, then the wet rattle she always tried to hide.\\n\\nWe didn’t have a baby. I used the monitor to hear my father if he fell.\\n\\n“Bad wiring,” he said when I asked.\\n\\nThat evening, I found him unplugging it with oven mitts.\\n\\n“Don’t answer her,” he told me.\\n\\nAt midnight, the receiver lit up inside my bedside drawer.\\n\\n“Your father needs his tablets.”\\n\\nI went downstairs. His pill organizer was empty, though I had filled it that morning.\\n\\nHe watched me from his armchair.\\n\\n“She used to say that,” he whispered.\\n\\nThe next night, the monitor said his bath was ready.\\n\\nWater seeped beneath the bathroom door. The taps were running. In the steam, someone had drawn a heart on the mirror, then rubbed it out.\\n\\nMy father packed a suitcase.\\n\\n“She was ill,” he said. “You know how ill she was.”\\n\\nI hadn’t asked him anything.\\n\\nThe monitor crackled.\\n\\n“Tell her which tablets.”\\n\\nHe sat on the suitcase until its lid buckled.\\n\\n“Tell her how many.”\\n\\nI looked toward the bathroom. The water had stopped.\\n\\nFor the first time since the funeral, I could smell my mother’s lavender soap.\\n\\nMy father grabbed my wrist.\\n\\n“Please,” he said. “She asked me to.”\\n\\nFrom the receiver came a long, painful effort to breathe.\\n\\nThen my mother said, quite clearly, “No.”\\n\\nThe bathroom door opened behind him.\\n\\nI had been holding the monitor’s talk button without realizing it.\\n\\n“Your bath is ready, Dad,” I said.\"\n}",
